funcrunch wrote:
> On May 13, 2:02 pm, Derek Ray <moot@just_a_spamtrap_anyway.com> wrote:
>> funcrunch wrote:
>>> Any other comments on my original idea of the no-quest-artifact-wish
>>> conduct?
>> I'm not sure there's a significant "ethical" difference between wishing
>> for quest artifacts and wishing for ordinary artifacts, given that
>> Grayswandir and Magicbane are both non-quest artifacts and excellent
>> wish targets.
> 
> For me the distinction is that a quest artifact is meant specifically
> for that character class. The fact that only Tourists can perform
> blessed charging with the PYEC illustrates this intention, in my
> opinion. 

And it also illustrates implicit support for wishing for quest
artifacts; if only a Tourist could obtain the PYEC, then there would be
no need to add any code restricting blessed charging to only Tourists.

By and large, though, there are still artifacts that you can wish for
that are noticeably better than certain quest artifacts; Magicbane
compared to the Heart of Ahriman, for example.  (Someone wishing for the
Heart should almost incur a positive conduct, frankly.)

> Now, rather than similarly crippling other quest artifacts
> for members not of the intended class (not sure how this would even
> work with the Eye or the Orb of Fate, probably the most desired quest
> artifacts from my reading others' ascension posts), I'm suggesting
> making it an optional conduct to get by without them.

Actually, I'd like to see more quest artifacts crippled in this fashion,
to provide a bit more challenge and variety for each class.  Mostly I'd
prefer to just increase variety, as there's no need to make the game
harder for anyone except a small handful of players.

The concept of "conducts", as applied to game balance, is largely
meaningless since they will simply be ignored by those who want the
artifact anyway.  When it's possible to make most any neutral character
an effective caster by bolting on SDSM, a robe, the Eye and the Eyes, it
renders the game itself somewhat silly, don't you think?  (Even when
taken in the context of itself.)

I think conducts are cute, don't get me wrong, and those who wish to
abide by them are welcome to.  But in discussions like this, I prefer to
balance the _real_ game, not the game with artificial user-voluntary
handicaps applied to it.  Thus, I'm in favor of adding no more
artificial voluntary handicaps, and instead taking a stab at curbing
some of the "Do this with every character to win" factors that currently
exist... one of those being "Go neutral, wish for the Eye, and be a
caster because spellcasting is borderline broken and even more so with
the Eye's power regen."

So:
IF some Quest Artifacts are too powerful when wished for,
AND 'too powerful' is definable in a useful fashion,
AND a change is available that increases variety without difficulty,
THEN the appropriate change is not to add a conduct, but decrease the
efficacy of wishing for Quest Artifacts.

